from .L import L
LESSONS={
"e-ft":L(
"Fine-tuning is extra training on your own examples so a model learns a format, style or narrow skill. It's like on-the-job training after general schooling. It's powerful but often unnecessary: better prompts or better retrieval solve many problems more cheaply. Fine-tune when you've hit their ceiling and have good data.",
["Prove the ceiling: measure the best prompt + RAG setup on your eval set first.",
 "Collect and clean examples: a few hundred to a few thousand high-quality input-output pairs, checked for licensing and PII.",
 "Choose a method: SFT on demonstrations; LoRA or QLoRA for efficient adapters; DPO-style methods when you have preferred vs rejected answers.",
 "Train, then evaluate on a held-out set plus general-capability regression tests.",
 "Version datasets and checkpoints, keep a rollback, and record why fine-tuning beat prompting."],
"LoRA parameter count: adapting one 4096 × 4096 attention projection with rank r = 16 trains two matrices, 4096 × 16 and 16 × 4096, which is 2 × 65,536 = 131,072 parameters instead of 16.8M, about 0.8%. Applying it to the 4 attention projections in each of 32 layers gives 128 × 131,072 ≈ 16.8M trainable parameters for the whole model.",
["Fine-tuning to fix what is actually a retrieval problem: the model can't learn facts that change weekly.","Training on data you don't have the rights to use.","Skipping general regression tests and causing the model to forget other skills."],
"An insurer fine-tuned a small open model with LoRA on 3,000 approved claim-summary examples. It matched a much larger API model's quality on their eval at about a tenth of the serving cost, and the adapter can be rolled back in minutes.",
["Prompt and RAG first; fine-tune at the ceiling.","Data quality beats quantity.","LoRA makes adaptation cheap and reversible."],
"flowchart LR\n  BASE[Best prompt + RAG] --> EV{Ceiling reached?}\n  EV -->|no| ITER[Improve prompt / retrieval]\n  EV -->|yes| DATA[Curate examples]\n  DATA --> M{Method}\n  M --> SFT[SFT]\n  M --> LORA[LoRA / QLoRA]\n  M --> DPO[DPO]\n  SFT --> TEST[Held-out + regression evals]\n  LORA --> TEST\n  DPO --> TEST\n  TEST --> SHIP[Ship with rollback]",
"Diagram: start from the best prompt and RAG; if the ceiling is reached, curate examples, choose SFT, LoRA or DPO, run held-out and regression evals, and ship with rollback.",
{"e-ft-when-s1":"Run your eval with the best prompt and retrieval you can build. If they already meet the target, don't fine-tune.",
 "e-ft-when-s4":"Narrow fine-tuning can degrade unrelated abilities; keep general tests in the eval suite.",
 "e-ft-how-s2":"LoRA trains small low-rank matrices added to frozen weights; QLoRA does this on a 4-bit quantized base model to save memory.",
 "e-ft-how-s3":"DPO learns directly from pairs of preferred and rejected answers without training a separate reward model.",
 "e-ft-ops-s2":"Evaluate several checkpoints, not just the last one, because later checkpoints can overfit."},widget="quant"),

"e-synth":L(
"Good training data is scarce, so teams ask a strong 'teacher' model to generate examples for a smaller 'student'. Done carefully, synthetic data and distillation let a small, cheap model do one job almost as well as a big one. Done carelessly, the student inherits the teacher's mistakes and your evals become meaningless.",
["Define the target task and a small set of real, human-checked gold examples.",
 "Generate candidates with a teacher model using varied prompts, personas and difficulty levels.",
 "Filter: automatic checks, a critique model and human spot checks remove bad examples.",
 "Train the student (SFT or distillation) and evaluate on held-out real data, not synthetic data.",
 "Check for contamination: synthetic examples must not copy your eval questions."],
"Filtering yield: you generate 20,000 candidate Q&A pairs. Deduplication removes 15% (17,000 left), schema and fact checks remove 25% of the rest (12,750 left), and a critique model rejects 20% (10,200 left). Human review of a 300-example sample finds 4% still bad, so you expect about 400 bad examples in the final set and decide whether to tighten filters.",
["Reporting gains only on synthetic test sets.","Low diversity: thousands of near-identical examples that teach one narrow pattern.","Ignoring the teacher model's terms of use on training other models."],
"A customer-support company distills a large model's ticket-classification behavior into a small model using 50,000 synthetic tickets plus 2,000 real labeled ones. On a held-out real set, the student is within 1.5 points of the teacher at a fraction of the latency.",
["Synthetic data needs filters and real held-out gold.","Diversity matters as much as volume.","Students inherit teacher blind spots."],
"flowchart LR\n  GOLD[Real gold examples] --> T[Teacher prompts: varied]\n  T --> GEN[Generate candidates]\n  GEN --> F1[Dedup + checks]\n  F1 --> F2[Critique model]\n  F2 --> F3[Human spot check]\n  F3 --> TRAIN[Train student]\n  TRAIN --> EVAL[Eval on held-out real data]\n  GOLD --> EVAL",
"Diagram: real gold examples guide varied teacher prompts; generated candidates pass deduplication, critique and human checks; a student is trained and evaluated on held-out real data.",
{"e-synth-gen-s2":"Vary prompts, topics, personas, lengths and difficulty so the data covers the real distribution.",
 "e-synth-gen-s3":"Have a model critique or verify each example and discard failures before training.",
 "e-synth-gen-s4":"Your own agent's real traces, corrected by humans, often teach more than generic synthetic chats.",
 "e-synth-dist-s1":"Logit distillation matches the teacher's full probability distribution; response distillation trains on the teacher's text outputs.",
 "e-synth-dist-s4":"If the teacher is wrong on a type of input, the student will be too, so test those cases separately."}),

"e-docrag":L(
"Enterprise knowledge lives in PDFs, slides, scanned forms, tables and charts, not just clean text. Plain text extraction loses table structure and ignores charts, so answers hiding there are never found. Document RAG either parses the layout carefully before indexing, or retrieves page images directly with vision models, and often does both.",
["Parse: a layout-aware parser finds headings, paragraphs, tables, figures and reading order.",
 "Convert tables to Markdown or HTML with headers repeated, and describe charts and images with a vision model.",
 "Chunk with page numbers and bounding boxes so citations can point to the exact region.",
 "Optionally index page images with a ColPali-style model for visual retrieval, and fuse with text retrieval.",
 "Generate with a VLM or LLM that cites page and region, and evaluate by question type (prose, table, chart)."],
"Visual index size: ColPali-style models store about 1,030 patch vectors of 128 dimensions per page. For 100,000 pages in float16: 100,000 × 1,030 × 128 × 2 bytes ≈ 26 GB. Binarizing vectors (1 bit per dimension) cuts that to about 1.6 GB, which is why compression or a first-stage single-vector filter is common.",
["Flattening tables into text so rows and columns get mixed up.","Losing page numbers, so answers can't be cited or verified.","Evaluating only on prose questions when users ask about tables and charts."],
"An investment firm indexes 10 years of annual reports. Parsing tables with headers preserved and adding chart descriptions raised accuracy on financial-table questions from 52% to 81% on their eval; citations highlight the exact table on the page.",
["Layout matters: parse structure before chunking.","Keep page and box references for citations.","Evaluate per question type."],
"flowchart LR\n  PDF[PDF / scan] --> LP[Layout parser]\n  LP --> TXT[Text chunks]\n  LP --> TAB[Tables as Markdown + headers]\n  LP --> FIG[Charts -> VLM description]\n  PDF --> IMG[Page images] --> VIS[(Visual index)]\n  TXT --> TI[(Text index)]\n  TAB --> TI\n  FIG --> TI\n  TI --> FUSE[Fuse + rerank]\n  VIS --> FUSE\n  FUSE --> GEN[Answer with page + box cites]",
"Diagram: a PDF is parsed into text, tables and chart descriptions for a text index, page images go to a visual index, results are fused and reranked, and answers cite page and box.",
{"e-docrag-t1-s0":"Open-source parsers (Docling, Unstructured) and services (LlamaParse, Azure Document Intelligence, AWS Textract, Google Document AI) differ on tables, scans and cost. Test them on your documents.",
 "e-docrag-t1-s2":"Table structure recognition recovers rows, columns and merged headers so values keep their meaning.",
 "e-docrag-t1-s3":"Repeat the header row in every chunk of a long table so each chunk is self-explanatory.",
 "e-docrag-t2-s1":"ColPali embeds each image patch and scores a query by matching each query token to its best patch (late interaction).",
 "e-docrag-t2-s5":"Visual retrieval shines on slides, charts and complex layouts where parsing loses information.",
 "e-docrag-t3-s2":"Reciprocal rank fusion merges ranked lists by summing 1/(k + rank) per item, rewarding agreement.",
 "e-docrag-t3-s5":"Higher image resolution means more image tokens, so pick the smallest resolution that keeps text readable."}),

"e-vision":L(
"Many business processes start with a picture: a receipt, an invoice, an ID card, a form. The goal is not to describe the picture but to turn it into trustworthy data, such as a vendor name, a date and a total that adds up. Vision models make extraction flexible, and validation rules make it reliable.",
["Preprocess the image: rotate, deskew, check resolution and blur.",
 "Extract with OCR plus rules, a prebuilt document processor, or a VLM with a strict JSON schema.",
 "Allow null for missing fields instead of guessing, and capture evidence (text or box) for each field.",
 "Validate: arithmetic (line items sum to subtotal, subtotal + tax = total), formats, known vendors, currency.",
 "Route failures or low-confidence fields to human review and track the straight-through processing rate."],
"Validation catches a hallucination: a VLM reads a receipt as subtotal 42.50, tax 3.40, total 48.90. The check 42.50 + 3.40 = 45.90 ≠ 48.90 fails, so the receipt goes to review. A human sees the total is actually 45.90 (the model misread a 5 as an 8). Storing amounts as integer cents (4250 + 340 = 4590) avoids floating-point rounding issues.",
["Trusting one extraction without arithmetic or format checks.","Storing money as floating-point numbers.","Testing only on clean scans when users send blurry phone photos."],
"An accounts-payable team processes 40,000 invoices a month. A prebuilt invoice processor handles common layouts, a VLM handles the long tail, and validation rules send 8% to human review. Straight-through processing rose from 35% to 78%.",
["Extraction is a guess; validation makes it data.","Prefer null to a plausible guess.","Measure straight-through rate and field accuracy."],
"flowchart LR\n  IMG[Photo / scan] --> PRE[Preprocess]\n  PRE --> EX{Extractor}\n  EX --> OCR[OCR + rules]\n  EX --> PB[Prebuilt processor]\n  EX --> VLM[VLM + JSON schema]\n  OCR --> VAL[Validate: sums, formats, vendors]\n  PB --> VAL\n  VLM --> VAL\n  VAL -->|pass| DB[(Records)]\n  VAL -->|fail| HR[Human review]",
"Diagram: a photo is preprocessed and extracted by OCR, a prebuilt processor or a VLM; results are validated and either saved or sent to human review.",
{"e-vision-t1-s2":"Fixing rotation, contrast and skew before extraction often improves accuracy more than changing the model.",
 "e-vision-t1-s4":"Cloud services such as Textract AnalyzeExpense or Azure prebuilt invoice models return typed fields with confidence scores.",
 "e-vision-t2-s2":"Tell the model to return null when a field isn't visible; a missing value is safer than an invented one.",
 "e-vision-t3-s2":"Use integer minor units (cents) or decimal types for money, never binary floats.",
 "e-vision-t3-s5":"Straight-through processing rate is the share of documents that need no human touch, a key business metric."}),

"e-multi":L(
"Not every question needs your most expensive model. Routing sends easy requests to cheap models and hard ones to strong models, like a hospital triage desk. Ensembles go the other way: spend more on hard cases by sampling several answers and picking the best with a verifier. Both are ways to buy quality where it matters and save money where it doesn't.",
["Collect signals: request features (length, topic), a small classifier, or the cheap model's own confidence.",
 "Route: rules or a learned router choose a model; a cascade tries cheap first and escalates on low confidence or verifier failure.",
 "For hard cases, use best-of-N sampling with a verifier (tests, schema, judge) or a critique step.",
 "Cap cost and latency per request so cascades and ensembles can't run away.",
 "Evaluate quality and cost together on the same slices, with honest A/B assignment."],
"Cascade economics: 1M requests at 1,500 tokens. Always large at $5 per 1M tokens: 1.5B tokens → $7,500. Cascade: all go to small at $0.30 per 1M ($450), and 20% escalate to large (300M tokens → $1,500). Total $1,950, a 74% saving, as long as routed quality stays within your threshold.",
["Training a router on demo prompts that don't look like production traffic.","Cascades with no cost ceiling, so hard requests try every model.","Comparing models on different traffic slices and calling it an A/B test."],
"A coding-help product sends autocomplete to a small fast model, chat questions to a mid model, and failing-test fixes to a reasoning model with best-of-3 and the test suite as verifier. Cost per active user fell 45% with higher fix rates.",
["Route cheap first; escalate on evidence.","Verifiers make best-of-N worth it.","Always cap cost and latency."],
"flowchart LR\n  R[Request] --> RT{Router}\n  RT -->|easy| S[Small model]\n  RT -->|hard| L[Large model]\n  S --> V{Verifier}\n  V -->|pass| OUT[Answer]\n  V -->|fail| L\n  L --> BON[Best-of-N + verifier]\n  BON --> OUT",
"Diagram: a router sends easy requests to a small model and hard ones to a large model; small-model answers that fail a verifier escalate, and the large model can use best-of-N with a verifier.",
{"e-multi-route-s1":"Heuristic routers use rules (length, keywords); learned routers are classifiers trained on which model succeeded for past requests.",
 "e-multi-route-s2":"Low token probabilities or high entropy in the cheap model's answer can signal it should escalate.",
 "e-multi-route-s3":"A cascade tries models in order of cost and stops at the first acceptable answer.",
 "e-multi-ens-s1":"Generate N answers and keep the one a verifier scores highest. Cost grows about linearly with N.",
 "e-multi-ens-s2":"A verifier can be code (tests, schema), a rule set or a judge model that checks the answer."},widget="routing"),

"e-datafly":L(
"A data flywheel turns everyday usage into improvements. Each interaction can produce evidence: a thumbs-down, an edit, an escalation. Captured carefully, that evidence grows your eval set, finds failure patterns and becomes training data, which makes the product better, which brings more usage. It needs consent, redaction and discipline to spin safely.",
["Capture consented, redacted traces: prompt, context, tool calls, output, model and prompt versions, feedback.",
 "Mine failures: cluster low-rated or escalated interactions and label root causes.",
 "Promote real failures into the golden eval set.",
 "Build training data: corrected answers for SFT, chosen/rejected pairs for DPO.",
 "Retrain or re-prompt, gate on evals, and repeat on a regular cadence."],
"From feedback to pairs: in a month, 200,000 answers get 6,000 thumbs-downs. In 2,500 of those, the user regenerated and accepted a new answer, giving 2,500 candidate chosen/rejected pairs. After PII scrubbing, deduplication and removing noisy or spammy feedback (30%), 1,750 pairs remain, enough for a small DPO run on a LoRA adapter.",
["Storing everything forever without consent or redaction.","Never promoting production failures into the eval set.","Training on raw feedback without filtering noise and abuse."],
"A writing-assistant product reviews the top 5 failure clusters every week. Each fix ships with new eval cases from those clusters, and accepted user edits feed a monthly preference-tuning run.",
["Instrument for learning from day one.","Failures become evals first, training data second.","Consent, redaction and filtering are non-negotiable."],
"flowchart LR\n  USE[Usage] --> CAP[Capture redacted traces + feedback]\n  CAP --> MINE[Mine failure clusters]\n  MINE --> EVAL[Grow eval set]\n  MINE --> PAIRS[SFT data + DPO pairs]\n  PAIRS --> TRAIN[Train / re-prompt]\n  EVAL --> GATE{Eval gate}\n  TRAIN --> GATE\n  GATE --> SHIP[Ship] --> USE",
"Diagram: usage is captured as redacted traces and feedback, failures are mined into eval cases and training pairs, training is gated by evals, and shipped improvements bring more usage.",
{"e-fly-cap-s1":"Get user and legal consent for using interactions to improve the product, and strip personal data before storage.",
 "e-fly-cap-s5":"Users who give feedback are not a random sample; weigh this when interpreting feedback rates.",
 "e-fly-use-s1":"Every confirmed production failure should become a test case so it never silently returns.",
 "e-datafly-x1-s2":"A thumbs-down followed by an accepted regeneration or edit gives a natural rejected/chosen pair.",
 "e-datafly-x1-s5":"Promote a new adapter only if it beats the current one on offline evals and in a shadow or canary test."}),

"e-edge":L(
"Running a model on the user's own device means no network round trip, no per-token bill and data that never leaves the phone or laptop. The price is a much smaller model, tight memory, battery use and a harder update story. Edge AI works best for narrow, frequent tasks, often with the cloud as a backup for harder ones.",
["Pick a small model and quantize it (for example to 4-bit GGUF) to fit the device's memory.",
 "Choose a runtime for the hardware: llama.cpp, MLX on Apple silicon, ExecuTorch or vendor NPU SDKs.",
 "Budget memory: weights + KV cache + app, below what the OS allows.",
 "Design hybrid behavior: local first, cloud fallback with user consent for harder requests.",
 "Ship updates for models and prompts safely, and test on real target devices."],
"Memory check: a 3B-parameter model at 4-bit is about 3e9 × 0.5 bytes = 1.5 GB of weights (plus some overhead, call it ~1.8 GB). On a phone with 8 GB RAM where an app can realistically use 2-3 GB, it fits with a small KV cache; a 7B model at 4-bit (~3.5-4 GB) would likely be killed by the OS on mid-range phones.",
["Promising 'fully offline' while telemetry or features still need the network.","Testing only on a developer laptop instead of mid-range target devices.","Ignoring battery and thermal throttling during long generations."],
"A field-service app runs a small on-device model to summarize technician notes and search manuals offline in basements with no signal. When connectivity returns, complex diagnostic questions can be sent to a cloud model with the technician's consent.",
["Small + quantized + narrow task = good edge fit.","Budget memory for weights and KV cache.","Hybrid local/cloud with honest privacy copy."],
"flowchart LR\n  U[User request] --> LOC[On-device model: 4-bit]\n  LOC --> C{Confident / in scope?}\n  C -->|yes| OUT[Answer locally]\n  C -->|no| CONSENT{User consents?}\n  CONSENT -->|yes| CLOUD[Cloud model]\n  CONSENT -->|no| DEG[Degraded answer]\n  CLOUD --> OUT",
"Diagram: a request goes to an on-device 4-bit model; if it isn't confident, the app asks for consent to use a cloud model or returns a degraded answer.",
{"e-edge-stack-s1":"GGUF is the file format used by llama.cpp for quantized models, commonly at 4-8 bits per weight.",
 "e-edge-stack-s3":"Total memory is weights plus KV cache plus runtime overhead; long contexts grow the KV cache.",
 "e-edge-ux-s1":"Loading a model from storage takes time. Load lazily, keep it warm when appropriate, and show progress.",
 "e-edge-ux-s3":"Tell users plainly what stays on the device and what may be sent to the cloud."},widget="quant"),

"e-llmops":L(
"LLMOps is the platform work that lets many teams ship agents safely without each one reinventing the plumbing. Most teams don't train models; they combine prompts, tools, retrieval and model choices. So the versioned, tested, deployable artifacts are those combinations, released through eval gates, watched in production and able to degrade gracefully when things break.",
["Provide a gateway with model aliases, keys, budgets, fallbacks and caching.",
 "Keep registries for prompts, tools/MCP servers and agent manifests, with owners and versions.",
 "Release through CI/CD with eval gates against baselines, then canary with online evals and automatic rollback.",
 "Store release evidence: eval results, approvals and versions.",
 "Define fallback chains and cost kill switches, and test them with chaos drills."],
"Fallback chain math: 1B tokens a month all on a frontier model at $10 per 1M costs $10,000. In degraded mode after an 80% budget alert: 30% cache hits ($0), 50% mid-tier at $1 per 1M ($500), 20% frontier for critical features ($2,000), total $2,500. If the frontier provider is down 2% of the time and the mid-tier provider 1%, independently, both are down 0.02 × 0.01 = 0.02% of the time.",
["Pinning nothing, so a provider's silent model update changes behavior.","Releasing prompt changes without eval gates because 'it's just text'.","Fallbacks that were never tested and fail when needed."],
"A bank's platform team offers an agent template: gateway access with model aliases, a prompt registry, an eval CI job and a canary deploy. A prompt change that increased refusals by 8% was rolled back automatically within 20 minutes of canary.",
["Version the whole agent: prompts, tools, models, configs.","Eval gates and canaries for every release.","Plan and drill degradation."],
"flowchart LR\n  MAN[Agent manifest: prompt v, tools v, model alias] --> CI[CI eval gate]\n  CI --> CAN[Canary + online evals]\n  CAN -->|ok| PROD[Production]\n  CAN -->|regression| RB[Auto rollback]\n  PROD --> GW[Gateway]\n  GW --> F[Frontier]\n  GW -.fallback.-> M[Mid-tier]\n  GW -.fallback.-> LOC[Local]\n  GW -.fallback.-> CACHE[Cache]",
"Diagram: an agent manifest passes a CI eval gate and a canary with online evals to production or rollback; production calls go through a gateway with fallbacks from frontier to mid-tier, local and cache.",
{"e-llmops-t1-s2":"A model alias such as 'support-default' points at a pinned version, so you can change models in one place with an eval run.",
 "e-llmops-t1-s5":"An agent manifest lists the prompt, tool, model and policy versions that make up one releasable agent.",
 "e-llmops-t2-s2":"Compare each candidate release with the current baseline on the eval suite and block it if key metrics drop.",
 "e-llmops-t2-s4":"If online metrics regress during a canary, automatically route traffic back to the previous version.",
 "e-llmops-x1-s2":"Fall back on errors, timeouts, rate limits or budget thresholds, and log which tier served each request.",
 "e-llmops-x1-s4":"Tell users when answers come from a reduced mode so they can calibrate trust."},widget="routing"),

"e-voice":L(
"A voice agent has to listen, think and speak fast enough to feel like a conversation. People notice gaps longer than about a second. So voice is mostly a latency and turn-taking problem: knowing when the user has finished, starting to speak before the whole answer is ready, and stopping instantly when the user interrupts.",
["Choose an architecture: cascaded (streaming speech-to-text → LLM with tools → streaming text-to-speech) or a speech-to-speech realtime model.",
 "Detect speech and end of turn with voice activity detection and, ideally, a semantic end-of-turn model.",
 "Stream: send partial transcripts, stream LLM tokens and start TTS at the first sentence.",
 "Handle barge-in: when the user speaks, stop audio, cancel generation and trim context to what was actually spoken.",
 "Integrate telephony (SIP, Twilio media streams at 8 kHz), transfers, DTMF and consent notices."],
"Latency budget for a cascaded agent: end-of-turn detection 300 ms + final STT 150 ms + LLM time to first token 400 ms + time to first sentence 200 ms + TTS time to first audio 150 ms + network 100 ms = 1,300 ms. To get under 1 s, shorten end-of-turn delay with a semantic model, use a faster LLM, and start TTS on the first clause.",
["Waiting for the full LLM answer before starting TTS.","No barge-in, so the agent talks over the user.","Keeping the unspoken part of an interrupted answer in context, so the agent thinks it said things it didn't."],
"A clinic's appointment line uses a cascaded voice agent with tools for calendar lookup. It plays a short filler ('Let me check that') when a tool call takes longer than 700 ms, transfers to staff on request, and keeps transcripts for quality review with patient consent.",
["Latency and turn-taking decide whether voice feels natural.","Stream every stage; start speaking early.","Barge-in must cancel audio, generation and context."],
"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as Caller\n  participant V as VAD / end-of-turn\n  participant S as STT\n  participant L as LLM + tools\n  participant T as TTS\n  U->>V: speech\n  V->>S: end of turn\n  S->>L: transcript\n  L->>T: first sentence (streaming)\n  T-->>U: audio starts\n  U->>V: interrupts (barge-in)\n  V->>T: stop audio\n  V->>L: cancel + trim context",
"Sequence diagram: caller speech goes through voice activity detection to speech-to-text, the LLM streams its first sentence to text-to-speech, audio starts, and a user interruption stops audio and cancels generation.",
{"e-voice-t1-s1":"Each stage can be swapped and inspected, and you keep full control of the LLM and tools.",
 "e-voice-t1-s2":"A single model takes audio in and produces audio out, keeping tone and reducing latency, with less control over each step.",
 "e-voice-t2-s1":"VAD detects when someone is speaking, based on audio energy and learned patterns.",
 "e-voice-t2-s2":"Semantic end-of-turn models look at what was said to tell a pause from a finished thought.",
 "e-voice-t3-s1":"Add up each stage's delay and target well under a second from the user stopping to the agent starting to speak.",
 "e-voice-t3-s4":"Phone audio is narrowband (8 kHz), which lowers transcription accuracy; test with real call audio."}),

"e-post":L(
"Post-training is what turns a raw pretrained model into a helpful assistant or a strong agent. It usually runs in stages: supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations, preference optimization from human or AI comparisons, and reinforcement learning on tasks with checkable answers. Application engineers don't need to run it alone, but they need to speak the language and bring clean environments and evals.",
["SFT: train on high-quality demonstrations of the desired behavior.",
 "Preference optimization: RLHF with a reward model and PPO, or direct methods such as DPO, using chosen/rejected pairs.",
 "RL with verifiable rewards: run the model on tasks (math, code, tool use) and reward correct outcomes, often with GRPO.",
 "Evaluate every stage for capability gains and safety or general-skill regressions.",
 "Decide whether training is the right fix at all, versus changing the product, prompt or tool."],
"GRPO's group-relative advantage: for one prompt the model samples 4 answers with rewards [1, 0, 0, 1]. Mean = 0.5, standard deviation = 0.5. Advantages = (r − mean)/std = [+1, −1, −1, +1]. Correct answers are pushed up and wrong ones down, without training a separate value model.",
["Asking for RL to fix what is really a tool-schema or prompt bug.","Optimizing a proxy reward that the model learns to game.","Evaluating on data that leaked into training."],
"An enterprise software company works with its model provider on reinforcement fine-tuning for a code-migration agent. It supplies 2,000 tasks in a sandbox with test suites as rewards, plus held-out tasks for evaluation; pass rate on held-out tasks rose from 41% to 63%.",
["SFT → preferences → RL with verifiable rewards.","Rewards get gamed; audit trajectories.","Often the right fix isn't training."],
"flowchart LR\n  PT[Pretrained model] --> SFT[SFT on demonstrations]\n  SFT --> PREF[Preference optimization: DPO / RLHF]\n  PREF --> RL[RL with verifiable rewards: GRPO]\n  RL --> EV[Capability + safety evals]\n  EV --> REL[Release]",
"Diagram: a pretrained model goes through supervised fine-tuning, preference optimization, reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, evaluation and release.",
{"e-post-map-s2":"RLHF trains a reward model on human comparisons and optimizes the policy against it; RLAIF uses AI feedback instead of humans.",
 "e-post-map-s4":"GRPO compares a group of sampled answers per prompt and uses their relative rewards, avoiding a separate value network.",
 "e-post-risk-s2":"Reward hacking is when the model finds a way to get high reward without doing the task, such as editing the tests.",
 "e-post-risk-s5":"If the problem is missing data, a bad tool or unclear instructions, fix that instead of training.",
 "e-post-x1-s3":"Verifiable rewards come from checks like unit tests passing or an exact answer match."}),

"e-multimodal-app":L(
"Accepting images, PDFs and audio changes your app's contract. Files can be huge, malicious, low quality or full of hidden instructions, and each modality needs its own processing and evaluation. Good multimodal products put gates and pipelines in the API, use the cheapest path that works (often text extraction) and test on the messy inputs real users send.",
["Gate uploads: size limits, MIME allowlists, malware scanning.",
 "Route by modality: text extraction or OCR first for documents, VLM fallback for complex layouts, STT for audio, screenshot understanding for UIs.",
 "Treat all extracted content as untrusted input (it can contain prompt injection).",
 "Build modality-specific eval sets, including photos of pages, noisy audio and adversarial images.",
 "Design accessible outputs, such as alt text and transcripts."],
"Choosing the cheaper path: a 20-page digital PDF has extractable text of about 10,000 tokens. Sending it as 20 page images to a VLM might cost roughly 1,500 image tokens per page, 30,000 tokens. Text extraction first is about 3× cheaper and only pages with figures need the VLM.",
["No upload size limit or file-type allowlist.","Evaluating only on clean digital PDFs when users send phone photos.","Letting text inside images change the agent's instructions or permissions."],
"An insurance claims app accepts photos, PDFs and voice notes. Uploads are scanned and size-limited, voice notes are transcribed, PDFs use text extraction with a VLM fallback for damage photos, and adjusters see transcripts and extracted fields side by side.",
["Gate files in the API, not the prompt.","Cheapest modality path first.","Eval on messy real inputs."],
"flowchart LR\n  UP[Upload] --> GATE[Size + MIME + malware]\n  GATE --> ROUTE{Modality}\n  ROUTE -->|PDF| TXT[Text extraction] --> VLMF[VLM fallback for figures]\n  ROUTE -->|image| VLM[VLM]\n  ROUTE -->|audio| STT[Speech-to-text]\n  TXT --> APP[App / agent: treat as untrusted]\n  VLMF --> APP\n  VLM --> APP\n  STT --> APP",
"Diagram: uploads pass size, type and malware gates, then are routed by modality to text extraction with VLM fallback, a VLM or speech-to-text, and the results enter the app as untrusted content.",
{"e-mm-pipe-s1":"Try text extraction or OCR first; it is cheaper and easier to cite than sending page images.",
 "e-mm-pipe-s5":"Reject files that are too large or of unexpected types before any processing.",
 "e-mm-eval-s1":"Build gold sets per modality, with the real quality and variety users send.",
 "e-mm-eval-s3":"Text hidden in images (tiny, faint or in screenshots) can carry instructions; never let it change permissions."}),

"e-rlenv":L(
"Reinforcement learning for agents is like building a flight simulator and a scoring system for a trainee pilot. The simulator (environment) gives realistic tasks and tools; the score (reward) says whether the task was really done. The model practices many times and learns what scores well, so if the simulator or score is flawed, it learns the flaw.",
["Build an environment with a Gym-style interface: reset gives a task, step runs an action, and the environment returns observations and rewards.",
 "Use verifiable rewards where possible: tests passing, correct database state, exact answers; use graders or reward models otherwise.",
 "Run many sandboxed rollouts in parallel with fast inference (for example vLLM inside frameworks like verl, OpenRLHF or TRL).",
 "Audit trajectories for reward hacking and compare reward with held-out evals.",
 "Consider cheaper alternatives first: prompt optimization (DSPy, GEPA) or model merging."],
"Difficulty calibration: if a task's pass rate is 0% or 100% across all samples, GRPO-style group advantages are zero and the task teaches nothing. With 8 samples per task, a pass rate around 25-75% gives the most signal. Of 5,000 candidate tasks, a team keeps the 2,300 whose initial pass rate falls between 10% and 90%.",
["Rewards the model can satisfy without doing the task, such as editable tests or checks that only look at output format.","Tasks that are all too easy or too hard, giving no learning signal.","Trusting rising reward without checking held-out evals."],
"A company building a spreadsheet agent creates 3,000 tasks with target workbook states as rewards, in sandboxes with read-only graders. Weekly trajectory audits caught the agent deleting rows to make a sum check pass; the grader now checks the full table.",
["The environment and reward define what is learned.","Prefer verifiable, hard-to-game rewards.","Audit trajectories; watch held-out evals."],
"flowchart LR\n  ENV[Environment: tasks + sandboxed tools] --> RO[Rollouts: policy acts]\n  RO --> RW[Reward: tests / graders]\n  RW --> ALG[RL update: GRPO / PPO]\n  ALG --> POL[Updated policy]\n  POL --> RO\n  RO --> AUD[Trajectory audits]\n  POL --> HO[Held-out evals]",
"Diagram: an environment provides tasks and tools, the policy rolls out actions, rewards come from tests or graders, an RL update produces a new policy, with trajectory audits and held-out evals alongside.",
{"e-rlenv-t1-s1":"A reset/step/reward API (from OpenAI Gym, now Gymnasium) makes environments interchangeable across RL frameworks.",
 "e-rlenv-t1-s4":"Keep tasks the model sometimes solves; always-solved and never-solved tasks give no learning signal.",
 "e-rlenv-t2-s1":"Common hacks include editing tests, hard-coding expected outputs and exploiting grader bugs.",
 "e-rlenv-t2-s3":"Keep tests hidden from the policy and graders read-only so they can't be modified during rollouts.",
 "e-rlenv-t3-s1":"DSPy optimizers tune prompts and few-shot examples automatically against a metric, with no weight updates.",
 "e-rlenv-t3-s5":"Mergekit combines the weights of fine-tuned models of the same architecture to blend their skills."}),

"e-genmedia":L(
"Image and video generators start from pure noise and clean it up step by step until a picture matching the prompt appears, like a sculptor removing marble. In products they power marketing images, design variations and video drafts, and they bring their own costs, safety checks, likeness and copyright questions, and the need to label AI-made content.",
["Training: add noise to real images in many steps (forward process) and train a network to predict the noise (reverse process).",
 "Latent diffusion runs this in a compressed latent space from an autoencoder, which is much cheaper than pixels.",
 "Generation: start from noise and denoise in a number of sampler steps, guided by the text prompt (classifier-free guidance).",
 "Control outputs with image-to-image, inpainting, ControlNet conditioning or LoRA fine-tunes for a brand or product.",
 "In production: safety filters, consent for likenesses, provenance (C2PA) and cost tracking per approved asset."],
"Cost per approved asset: generating an image costs $0.04 and a reviewer approves 1 in 5, so model cost per approved image is $0.20. Adding review time of 30 seconds per candidate at $40 per hour (≈ $0.33 per candidate) makes the true cost ≈ 5 × ($0.04 + $0.33) ≈ $1.87 per approved image. Better prompts or a LoRA that lift approval to 1 in 2 cut that to ≈ $0.75.",
["Counting only generation cost and ignoring human review time.","Generating images of real people without consent.","Publishing AI-generated media with no provenance or disclosure where it is required or expected."],
"A retailer generates lifestyle images of its products with a LoRA trained on approved product photos. Every image carries C2PA content credentials, passes a safety filter, and is logged with prompt and model version for audit.",
["Diffusion = learned denoising from noise to image.","Control methods make outputs on-brand.","Measure cost per approved asset; add provenance."],
"flowchart LR\n  N[Random noise] --> D1[Denoise step 1]\n  D1 --> D2[Denoise step ...]\n  D2 --> DN[Denoise step N]\n  P[Text prompt] -.guidance.-> D1\n  P -.guidance.-> D2\n  P -.guidance.-> DN\n  DN --> DEC[Decode latent to image]\n  DEC --> SAFE[Safety filter + C2PA]\n  SAFE --> REV[Human review]",
"Diagram: random noise is denoised over N steps guided by a text prompt, decoded from latent space into an image, passed through a safety filter with C2PA provenance, and reviewed by a human.",
{"e-genmedia-t1-s1":"The forward process adds Gaussian noise over many steps; the model learns to reverse each step by predicting the added noise.",
 "e-genmedia-t1-s4":"Classifier-free guidance blends predictions with and without the prompt; higher guidance follows the prompt more closely but can reduce variety.",
 "e-genmedia-t1-s5":"Samplers decide how to step from noise to image; distilled models can produce good images in just a few steps.",
 "e-genmedia-t2-s2":"ControlNet adds a conditioning input, such as a pose, edge map or depth map, to control composition.",
 "e-genmedia-t3-s3":"C2PA content credentials attach signed metadata describing how an asset was made; watermarks embed signals in the pixels."}),
}
